# Line endings are a property of the project, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this file, normalisation comes from each contributor's global git config —
# on Windows that is usually core.autocrlf=true, which is why every commit from a
# Windows machine emits "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings. Every tracked text
# blob in this repo is already stored LF, so `text=auto` changes nothing on disk; it
# just makes that the rule rather than an accident.
* text=auto

# RimWorld writes ModsConfig.xml with CRLF, and this fixture is a real one. It must
# reach the working tree with its CRLF intact on EVERY platform, because
# WritePathTests.Writer_reproduces_the_real_mods_config_byte_for_byte compares the
# writer's output against these exact bytes — the guard on the one file this
# application writes into someone's game.
#
# Without this line the fixture normalises to LF and that test fails on Linux and
# macOS while passing on Windows, where core.autocrlf hides the difference. That is
# exactly what the first cross-platform CI run found: 217 missing CR bytes.
fixtures/config/ModsConfig.xml text eol=crlf

*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.slnx      text
*.axaml     text
*.xml       text
*.json      text
*.md        text
*.yml       text
*.py        text
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png       binary
*.ico       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.svg       text
*.dll       binary
*.exe       binary
*.zip       binary
*.db        binary

# Screenshots are documentation, not source; keep them out of language statistics.
assets/screenshots/** linguist-documentation
